---
import SongPreview from "../components/SongPreview.svelte";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Song Preview" description="Preview your Deluge song arrangement — clips, instruments, playback.">
  <section class="page-section">
    <div class="page-header">
      <h1 class="page-title">Song Preview</h1>
      <p class="page-desc">Experimental browser playback of Deluge songs.</p>
      <details class="page-details">
        <summary>How it works &amp; limitations</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>Handy when XML filenames are just numbers and you can't remember which song is which.</li>
          <li>Works best with songs that have arrangements; clip-only tracks get a best-effort attempt (and sometimes that effort isn't very good).</li>
          <li>Patches and samples won't sound right, which is not being considered a bug. The goal is a quick reference for easy SD card management, not a 1:1 recreation.</li>
          <li>Some renditions sound surprisingly cool; others are unlistenable. It's a coin-toss.</li>
          <li>Sometimes things crackle or go silent as polyphony increases (hoping to resolve this).</li>
          <li>Some basic mixing controls have been provided, because 1) it's fun, 2) sometimes the rendered mix is all over the map and needs tweaking to be useful.</li>
          <li>Again: think of this as a quick utility for referencing as you organize your SD card. If you're reminded what the song is, it did its job.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
    </div>
    <SongPreview client:load />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-section {
    max-width: 960px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1.5rem;
  }
  .page-header {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .page-title {
    font-family: 'DM Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .page-desc {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
